  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday and Tuesday night: The Inland NW will remain smokey but\nrelatively quiet one more day. Broad westerly flow aloft\npersists across the region, but a trough starts to deepen along\nthe Pacific NW coast late tonight. Smoke from regional \nwildfires will continue to spread from west to east, still \ncoming from the more active near the Cascades and north-central \nWA. \n\nWednesday to Friday: The aforementioned upper trough will swing\neastward through the forecast area Wednesday through early \nThursday. Guided by a rather robust jet streak from the eastern \nPacific, this system will have strong upper support for \nconvection. Precipitable water values increase to around 1\" in \nadvance of this trough, plenty of moisture to support \nconvection. The potential limiting factor for convection is \nsmoke and its impact on reducing surface heating. At this stage,\nour forecast mindset is that the upper level support is robust \nenough to overpower the low level convective inhibition. High \nresolution ensemble guidance is generally in good agreement, \nhighlighting the east slopes of the Cascades, the northern \nmountains and north Idaho Panhandle as the most favorable \nlocations for thunderstorms. Thunderstorms may produce brief \ndownpours and small hail. More notable however is that fuels \nremain extremely dry, and any lightning strikes have the \npotential to start new wildfires. In addition, gusty and erratic\noutflow winds from thunderstorms could be dangerous around new \nand existing wildfires. For these reasons, we have issued a Red \nFlag Warning for Wednesday afternoon and evening for the \naforementioned areas. Showers and thunderstorms will still be \npossible on Thursday, but will be lower risk than Wednesday. \n\nFriday through Monday: The upper trough exits the region to our\nnorth and east. The Inland NW will remain under broad \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, with a a trough off the West Coast and\nhigh pressure over the southern US. Weak impulses will continue\nto rotate through this flow, bringing periods of 10-20% chance \nof showers and possibly thunderstorms generally favoring the \nmountains. Confidence is low in timing out any of these \nprecipitation chances, and forcing is mostly on the weak side.\nForecast temperatures should rebound to the 80s to lower 90s,\ngenerally within a few degrees of normal.\n\nDang",
